response:
During the 1854 Soho cholera outbreak, **John Snow** identified the immediate source as **the public water pump on Broad Street**.

Supporting observations from the article include that:

- he **“found that nearly all the deaths had taken place within a short distance of the [Broad Street] pump”**;
- there were **“only ten deaths in houses situated decidedly nearer to another street-pump”**;
- in **five** of those cases, families said they **“always sent to the pump in Broad Street”** because they preferred its water;
- and in **three other cases**, the deceased were **children who went to school near the pump**;
- he also reported **“61 instances”** where the deceased had drunk water from that pump **“either constantly or occasionally.”**

The important limitation was that **“Snow's chemical and microscope examination of a water sample from the Broad Street pump did not conclusively prove its danger.”**

As a result of his findings, Snow told the **Board of Guardians of St James's parish**, and **“the handle of the pump was removed on the following day.”**
